<markdown_cell>
# Comparing py-spy profiling modes: top vs record/flamegraph vs dump

> L3 investigation — picking the right mode for the question you're asking, and reading each mode's output without getting lost.
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## Purpose

py-spy has three main interaction modes for inspecting a running Python process: `top`, `record` (which produces a flamegraph), and `dump`. Each one answers a different question, and reaching for the wrong one tends to either drown you in data or hide the very thing you needed to see. This notebook walks through each mode against the same target program so the trade-offs sit side by side.
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## The target program

A small CPU-bound loop with a couple of named functions, so the flamegraph and `top` views actually have distinguishable frames to show. The `hot()` function is what we expect to dominate samples; `warm()` does less.
</markdown_cell>

<code_cell>
# target.py — what we'll profile
def hot():
    total = 0
    for _ in range(50_000_000):
        total += 1
    return total

def warm():
    total = 0
    for _ in range(5_000_000):
        total += 1
    return total

if __name__ == "__main__":
    while True:
        warm()
        warm()
        hot()
</code_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## Mode 1: live `top` — what's hot right now

`py-spy top --pid <PID>` attaches and shows a continuously updating view of the hottest functions. It exits when you press `q`. This is the right mode when you suspect a runaway loop and want to confirm before generating a file.
</markdown_cell>

<code_cell>
# Start the target in the background, then attach `top` for ~3 seconds
python target.py &
PID=$!
sleep 0.5
timeout 3 py-spy top --pid $PID || true   # timeout exits so the demo doesn't hang
kill $PID 2>/dev/null || true
</code_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## Reading the `top` output

The columns are: function name on the left, then `%own` (the share of samples that landed in that exact frame) and `%total` (the share for that frame plus everything underneath it on the stack). A function with high `%own` and low `%total` is a leaf hot spot. A function with low `%own` but high `%total` is mostly calling into something else.
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## Mode 2: `record` with flamegraph — shareable artifact

`py-spy record -o profile.svg --pid <PID>` samples over a window and writes a single SVG. Open it in a browser; wider rectangles mean more samples. This is the right mode when you need to share findings or come back to them later, and when you want to follow the call tree visually rather than as text.
</markdown_cell>

<code_cell>
# Record a 5-second flamegraph of the same target
python target.py &
PID=$!
sleep 0.5
py-spy record -o profile.svg --pid $PID --duration 5 || true
kill $PID 2>/dev/null || true
ls -lh profile.svg
</code_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## Reading the flamegraph

Each rectangle is a stack frame at a point in time. The x-axis is sample count (not time), and the y-axis is call-stack depth. The widest frame at the top is `hot()` — that's where most samples landed. Clicking a frame zooms in on it; the tooltip shows exact percentages. One quirk worth knowing: `time.sleep()` doesn't show up, because py-spy doesn't count idle time as busy.
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## Mode 3: `dump` — one-shot snapshot

`py-spy dump --pid <PID>` prints the current call stack of every thread and exits immediately. No sampling window, no output file. This is the right mode when a process appears stuck and you want to see *what it's waiting on right now* — for example, to confirm whether it's blocked on I/O, on a lock, or stuck in a tight loop.
</markdown_cell>

<code_cell>
# One-shot dump of the same target mid-loop
python target.py &
PID=$!
sleep 0.5
py-spy dump --pid $PID | head -40
kill $PID 2>/dev/null || true
</code_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## What `dump` actually shows

The output is one stack trace per thread. For our target, you'll see the main thread inside `hot()` and the interpreter frames above it. If you ran `dump` against a process blocked on a socket call, you'd see the wait at the top with the C-level frame visible — py-spy shows C extension frames if you pass `--native`.
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## Side-by-side comparison

| Mode | Best question it answers | Output type | Overhead | When to avoid |
|------|--------------------------|-------------|----------|---------------|
| `top` | "What is this process doing right now?" | Live terminal view, exits on `q` | Negligible | Long-running debugging across sessions (no artifact) |
| `record` | "Show me the call tree over time so I can dig in" | SVG flamegraph (or speedscope JSON) | Negligible during sampling | Quick one-line diagnosis (overkill) |
| `dump` | "Why is this process stuck right now?" | One stack snapshot per thread, no file | Negligible | Profiling hot paths (no aggregation) |
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## Choosing between them

For a process you can't restart, the natural order is: `top` to confirm the suspicion, `dump` to grab a single-thread stack if it looks stuck, and `record` to capture a flamegraph you can share. For a process you can re-run, `record --duration` against the same script twice is usually faster than chasing a live target with `top`.
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## What to verify next

One thing to check is what happens when sampling rate and process CPU saturate — py-spy defaults to 100 Hz, but on a busy process you may want to drop to 50 Hz to reduce sampling overhead. Another angle is the `--gil` flag, which only matters on CPython and changes nothing on PyPy or free-threaded builds. A third is whether combining `--format speedscope` with the existing kit's `speedscope` workflow gives you a tighter interactive debugging loop than the SVG flamegraph does.
</markdown_cell>